Increase tolerances to avoid failures in 4 tests:

> TestOperatorsCPU.test_grad_linalg_lstsq_cpu_float32
> TestOperatorsCPU.test_grad_linalg_lstsq_grad_oriented_cpu_float32
> Mismatched elements: 1 / 36 (2.8%)
> Greatest absolute difference: 1.1444091796875e-05 at index (0, 0, 2) (up to 1e-05 allowed)
> Greatest relative difference: 1.1728669051080942e-05 at index (0, 0, 2) (up to 1.3e-06 allowed)

> TestOperatorsCPU.test_jvp_linalg_lstsq_cpu_float32
> TestOperatorsCPU.test_jvp_linalg_lstsq_grad_oriented_cpu_float32
> Mismatched elements: 1 / 27 (3.7%)
> Greatest absolute difference: 2.86102294921875e-05 at index (0, 0, 2) (up to 1e-05 allowed)
> Greatest relative difference: 7.663269570912234e-06 at index (0, 0, 2) (up to 1.3e-06 allowed)

See https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/201834

Similar:

> TestCommonCPU.test_noncontiguous_samples_linalg_lstsq_cpu_complex64
> TestCommonCPU.test_noncontiguous_samples_linalg_lstsq_cpu_float32
> Mismatched elements: 1 / 36 (2.8%)
> Greatest absolute difference: 1.52587890625e-05 at index (1, 3, 1) (up to 1e-05 allowed)
> Greatest relative difference: 5.170789336261805e-06 at index (1, 3, 1) (up to 1.3e-06 allowed)
> Got different gradients for contiguous / non-contiguous inputs wrt input 0.

And likely also this:
> TestCommonCPU.test_noncontiguous_samples_pca_lowrank_cpu_complex64              
> Mismatched elements: 3 / 12 (25.0%)
> Greatest absolute difference: 0.9013921618461609 at index (0, 0, 1) (up to 0.04 allowed)
> Greatest relative difference: 1.1567119359970093 at index (0, 1, 1) (up to 0.04 allowed)

Author: Alexander Grund (TU Dresden)

